WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.371 --> 00:00:03.039
- I love reading inspirational quotes

00:00:03.039 --> 00:00:05.269
and Gandhi's quote really stuck with me.

00:00:05.269 --> 00:00:07.259
"Be the change you want
to see in the world."

00:00:07.259 --> 00:00:09.840
My friends, we all want the gold

00:00:09.840 --> 00:00:12.703
but we don't love the grind.

00:00:12.703 --> 00:00:13.536
What's up with that?

00:00:13.536 --> 00:00:15.658
So many people go after
mentors just because

00:00:15.658 --> 00:00:17.569
they're successful at something.

00:00:17.569 --> 00:00:21.374
They need to be successful at
what you are wanting to do.

00:00:21.374 --> 00:00:23.577
When my mental bandwidth is the highest,

00:00:23.577 --> 00:00:24.950
that's when I go into action mode.

00:00:24.950 --> 00:00:26.816
First thing in the morning, the first hour

00:00:26.816 --> 00:00:30.166
is me producing my best content, period.

00:00:30.166 --> 00:00:32.562
I'm the host of EOFire,
an award-winning podcast

00:00:32.562 --> 00:00:34.581
where I interview today's most inspiring

00:00:34.581 --> 00:00:37.271
and successful entrepreneurs
seven days a week.

00:00:37.271 --> 00:00:40.359
In four years, I've interviewed
over 1600 entrepreneurs,

00:00:40.359 --> 00:00:42.840
amassed over 40 million listens

00:00:42.840 --> 00:00:44.359
and have turned EOFire into a

00:00:44.359 --> 00:00:46.757
multi-million-dollar-a-year business.

00:00:46.757 --> 00:00:48.547
I've created two journals to help guide

00:00:48.547 --> 00:00:50.247
entrepreneurs to success:

00:00:50.247 --> 00:00:53.211
"The Freedom Journal" and
"The Mastery Journal."

00:00:53.211 --> 00:00:54.878
I'm John Lee Dumas and here are my

00:00:54.878 --> 00:00:57.045
top ten rules for success.

00:01:00.314 --> 00:01:03.481
(inspirational music)

00:01:10.574 --> 00:01:12.539
Five years ago, almost to the day,

00:01:12.539 --> 00:01:14.999
is when I first heard about
podcasting for the same reason.

00:01:14.999 --> 00:01:18.267
Because I was sick and tired
of Miley Cyrus on the radio.

00:01:18.267 --> 00:01:19.848
I even went to the sports radio,

00:01:19.848 --> 00:01:22.628
but I'm not a Padres or Chargers fan so

00:01:22.628 --> 00:01:25.090
I wasn't loving the
sports talk radio either.

00:01:25.090 --> 00:01:26.207
And these commercials were so long,

00:01:26.207 --> 00:01:28.390
so I found podcasting as a listener

00:01:28.390 --> 00:01:29.946
and I fell in love with the medium.

00:01:29.946 --> 00:01:32.389
I got it right away,
on-demand targeted content.

00:01:32.389 --> 00:01:35.198
You can listen to it and you
can really connect with people.

00:01:35.198 --> 00:01:37.248
And I started listening
to people like Pat Flynn

00:01:37.248 --> 00:01:38.743
who has now become my good friend.

00:01:38.743 --> 00:01:40.473
He's right here in San Diego.

00:01:40.473 --> 00:01:42.256
Andrew Warner, David Siteman Garland,

00:01:42.256 --> 00:01:43.470
these guys inspired me and I said,

00:01:43.470 --> 00:01:45.427
"Man, I don't have to
follow this traditional

00:01:45.427 --> 00:01:47.758
route that I thought I had to."

00:01:47.758 --> 00:01:49.478
Coming from a very traditional family,

00:01:49.478 --> 00:01:53.238
military, et cetera,
and my eyes were opened.

00:01:53.238 --> 00:01:54.620
And it still was a couple years before

00:01:54.620 --> 00:01:58.611
I made that leap but in September of 2012,

00:01:58.611 --> 00:02:01.487
that was the day that I
launched Entrepreneur On Fire.

00:02:01.487 --> 00:02:04.457
Here we are just 1000 episodes later now,

00:02:04.457 --> 00:02:05.428
which is crazy to think.

00:02:05.428 --> 00:02:06.261
- That is crazy.

00:02:06.261 --> 00:02:08.459
- Seven days a week and we average

00:02:08.459 --> 00:02:11.086
over one million unique listens per month

00:02:11.086 --> 00:02:13.538
in over 145 countries,
so it's really become

00:02:13.538 --> 00:02:16.560
a platform that reaches the world.

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:17.639
Which I love.

00:02:17.639 --> 00:02:19.008
- So when you think of just like,

00:02:19.008 --> 00:02:22.727
almost becoming a lawyer and
being in some of those moments,

00:02:22.727 --> 00:02:25.117
what was it that bubbled
over where you said

00:02:25.117 --> 00:02:26.428
"I just can't take it any more."

00:02:26.428 --> 00:02:27.261
Where you're just like "You know what,

00:02:27.261 --> 00:02:29.438
I have to just do something different."

00:02:29.438 --> 00:02:31.478
Because last time we
had a lot of folks that

00:02:31.478 --> 00:02:34.628
have quit their jobs, replaced income,

00:02:34.628 --> 00:02:37.849
and so if you're in that E
quadrant that we talked about

00:02:37.849 --> 00:02:39.075
on the cash flow quadrant.

00:02:39.075 --> 00:02:40.361
So what was it that just said, you know,

00:02:40.361 --> 00:02:42.755
"I can't take it anymore,
I'm just going to go for it."

00:02:42.755 --> 00:02:45.910
- For me, what was the
really big boil-over point

00:02:45.910 --> 00:02:47.489
is I was in commercial real estate

00:02:47.489 --> 00:02:49.860
and I did enjoy a lot of the profession,

00:02:49.860 --> 00:02:51.648
but the reality was this:

00:02:51.648 --> 00:02:54.288
I locked down a huge deal,

00:02:54.288 --> 00:02:57.118
and it was going to be
a big commission check

00:02:57.118 --> 00:03:00.068
and I was not excited about it.

00:03:00.068 --> 00:03:02.158
I was like "Man, I am not excited

00:03:02.158 --> 00:03:05.351
about locking down what's
going to turn out to be

00:03:05.351 --> 00:03:07.507
a five-figure check."

00:03:07.507 --> 00:03:09.828
Because I'm like "Now I've
got to write the contract up,

00:03:09.828 --> 00:03:11.608
I got to do this, I got to do that."

00:03:11.608 --> 00:03:13.275
And I'm sure a lot of people
in this room can resonate

00:03:13.275 --> 00:03:15.376
with either your current
or your past jobs,

00:03:15.376 --> 00:03:17.728
just not being excited about
what it's going to take

00:03:17.728 --> 00:03:19.648
to get to do what you need to do.

00:03:19.648 --> 00:03:21.909
And I said "If I'm not even
excited about bringing in

00:03:21.909 --> 00:03:24.490
this big commission, this
is as good as it gets.

00:03:24.490 --> 00:03:25.987
IS this the rest of my life,

00:03:25.987 --> 00:03:28.075
is this what the rest of my
life is going to be like,

00:03:28.075 --> 00:03:29.888
where I'm just looking at the watch,

00:03:29.888 --> 00:03:31.880
trying to make a beeline
home as soon as possible,

00:03:31.880 --> 00:03:34.169
locking down big deals, not even caring.

00:03:34.169 --> 00:03:35.179
Is that going to be my life?"

00:03:35.179 --> 00:03:37.019
And I said "That's it,

00:03:37.019 --> 00:03:39.580
I refuse to live a life on those terms,

00:03:39.580 --> 00:03:40.680
I want to take control."

00:03:40.680 --> 00:03:43.007
It had always been at the back of my mind,

00:03:43.007 --> 00:03:46.180
I'm loving listening to these interviews

00:03:46.180 --> 00:03:48.008
with inspiring entrepreneurs.

00:03:48.008 --> 00:03:50.865
Pat Flynn's podcast was twice a month,

00:03:50.865 --> 00:03:52.740
David Siteman Garland's once a week,

00:03:52.740 --> 00:03:54.952
Andrew Warner, once a week.

00:03:54.952 --> 00:03:56.982
And they were bringing on great guests

00:03:56.982 --> 00:03:57.932
and telling great stories,

00:03:57.932 --> 00:03:59.018
and I was listening to these interviews

00:03:59.018 --> 00:04:00.455
as I was driving to work being like

00:04:00.455 --> 00:04:02.007
"Man, I need more of these.

00:04:02.007 --> 00:04:05.095
Because I'm driving to five
days a week, hitting the gym,

00:04:05.095 --> 00:04:05.928
I need more."

00:04:05.928 --> 00:04:07.267
And I couldn't find it.

00:04:07.267 --> 00:04:09.856
And so the idea was in
the back of my mind.

00:04:09.856 --> 00:04:12.481
I love reading inspirational quotes,

00:04:12.481 --> 00:04:14.349
and Gandhi's quote really stuck with me.

00:04:14.349 --> 00:04:17.177
"Be the change you want
to see in the world."

00:04:17.177 --> 00:04:19.488
I wanted to see a
seven-day-a-week podcast.

00:04:19.488 --> 00:04:21.306
I didn't know if anybody
else wanted to see that,

00:04:21.306 --> 00:04:23.518
but I decided to stop just whining

00:04:23.518 --> 00:04:25.506
and complaining about the
fact that it didn't exist

00:04:25.506 --> 00:04:28.629
and step forward and make it exist.

00:04:28.629 --> 00:04:30.905
My friends, we all want the gold

00:04:30.905 --> 00:04:33.978
but we don't love the grind.

00:04:33.978 --> 00:04:35.645
What's up with that?

00:04:39.327 --> 00:04:40.160
I get it.

00:04:40.160 --> 00:04:41.930
We see entrepreneurs that we admire

00:04:41.930 --> 00:04:44.948
having these amazing
experiences around the world,

00:04:44.948 --> 00:04:48.537
we see that Olympian with
tears streaming down their face

00:04:48.537 --> 00:04:50.456
as the Stars and Stripes go up

00:04:50.456 --> 00:04:52.569
and "The Star-Spangled Banner" is playing.

00:04:52.569 --> 00:04:55.558
But my friends, that's just a snapshot

00:04:55.558 --> 00:04:58.139
of the success of their lives.

00:04:58.139 --> 00:05:00.379
You don't see that 10 years of failure

00:05:00.379 --> 00:05:01.438
that that entrepreneur went through

00:05:01.438 --> 00:05:02.271
to get to success.

00:05:02.271 --> 00:05:03.807
You don't see those four years of sweat

00:05:03.807 --> 00:05:07.696
that Olympian had to put
in to get that gold medal.

00:05:07.696 --> 00:05:09.258
No, we just want that gold medal.

00:05:09.258 --> 00:05:11.798
We don't even think about
the grind that it took

00:05:11.798 --> 00:05:14.097
to stand on that podium.

00:05:14.097 --> 00:05:16.108
It's time for a mindset
shift, Fire Nation.

00:05:16.108 --> 00:05:18.888
I've interviewed over 1400
successful entrepreneurs,

00:05:18.888 --> 00:05:20.749
and they all love the grind.

00:05:20.749 --> 00:05:23.034
You must love the grind.

00:05:23.034 --> 00:05:26.978
So set a goal, make it
big, make it meaningful,

00:05:26.978 --> 00:05:29.549
and then start the grind.

00:05:29.549 --> 00:05:31.128
Start it today.

00:05:31.128 --> 00:05:33.508
The smartest thing that I
really should say that I did

00:05:33.508 --> 00:05:35.311
and my biggest early success

00:05:35.311 --> 00:05:38.097
was making that realization
that I needed a mentor.

00:05:38.097 --> 00:05:40.221
Because again, I only
knew how to press Play

00:05:40.221 --> 00:05:42.129
on a podcast, nothing else.

00:05:42.129 --> 00:05:44.936
So I looked out there to the podcasters

00:05:44.936 --> 00:05:47.728
that were currently at
where I wanted to be.

00:05:47.728 --> 00:05:50.205
That's so critical, so many
people go after mentors

00:05:50.205 --> 00:05:52.389
just because they're
successful at something.

00:05:52.389 --> 00:05:56.259
They need to be successful at
what you are wanting to do.

00:05:56.259 --> 00:05:59.030
They need to be where you want to be.

00:05:59.030 --> 00:06:02.297
And for three months and a
heavy investment on my part,

00:06:02.297 --> 00:06:05.097
rightfully so, I was mentored by Jamie.

00:06:05.097 --> 00:06:09.830
And that's just what opened
up so many doors so quickly.

00:06:09.830 --> 00:06:12.709
- Okay now, we had a great
question from Facebook

00:06:12.709 --> 00:06:14.850
which was: If someone
is starting right now

00:06:14.850 --> 00:06:16.967
and they can't afford
that kind of investment

00:06:16.967 --> 00:06:21.388
to find a mentor or coach, what
do you suggest that they do?

00:06:21.388 --> 00:06:24.317
Because at the very beginning,
it seems like you have

00:06:24.317 --> 00:06:26.229
no doors and no connections.

00:06:26.229 --> 00:06:27.598
You're just like, yourself alone

00:06:27.598 --> 00:06:29.100
starting a podcast in your basement.

00:06:29.100 --> 00:06:31.016
- Yeah.
- What do you suggest to them?

00:06:31.016 --> 00:06:33.095
- So number one, I would say

00:06:33.095 --> 00:06:35.189
getting that one-on-one
mentor is critical.

00:06:35.189 --> 00:06:38.039
So if you know you can't afford X,

00:06:38.039 --> 00:06:39.900
think about what you actually can afford.

00:06:39.900 --> 00:06:41.961
What can you invest, what dollar amount,

00:06:41.961 --> 00:06:43.347
and then just go.

00:06:43.347 --> 00:06:44.758
For me, a great example, I have people

00:06:44.758 --> 00:06:46.750
that come to me now all the time,

00:06:46.750 --> 00:06:50.819
and say "John, can you
mentor me to be a podcaster?"

00:06:50.819 --> 00:06:52.866
And I don't even have a price anymore.

00:06:52.866 --> 00:06:54.790
I don't one-on-one mentor anymore,

00:06:54.790 --> 00:06:57.498
I focus on other parts of my business,

00:06:57.498 --> 00:07:00.238
But I'm able to say "I'm
sorry, I don't mentor

00:07:00.238 --> 00:07:02.018
but I have a couple great options for you.

00:07:02.018 --> 00:07:03.158
What's your price range?

00:07:03.158 --> 00:07:06.919
Let me introduce you, hand
you off to an amazing mentor

00:07:06.919 --> 00:07:09.070
who loves doing this, who's
great at what he does,

00:07:09.070 --> 00:07:12.268
or what she does, and then
you'll be off to the races."

00:07:12.268 --> 00:07:14.207
Fortune favors the
bold, so still reach out

00:07:14.207 --> 00:07:15.409
with that mentality.

00:07:15.409 --> 00:07:18.820
And also offer what kind of
value are you going to exchange?

00:07:18.820 --> 00:07:22.026
Maybe you're willing to
run their Twitter account

00:07:22.026 --> 00:07:24.717
and maybe you're willing
to create some images

00:07:24.717 --> 00:07:26.028
or some design for them.

00:07:26.028 --> 00:07:27.658
Think about the bartering that you can do,

00:07:27.658 --> 00:07:31.930
trading your time, which is
valuable, saving them time.

00:07:31.930 --> 00:07:35.460
There can be some really cool
things that you can exchange

00:07:35.460 --> 00:07:37.750
that doesn't have to necessarily
be dollars and cents.

00:07:37.750 --> 00:07:39.198
And then also I do want to add,

00:07:39.198 --> 00:07:42.314
there's also some great
group coaching out there

00:07:42.314 --> 00:07:43.625
that's a lot cheaper.

00:07:43.625 --> 00:07:46.599
And then, somebody that
you've interviewed as well,

00:07:46.599 --> 00:07:48.711
Lewis Howes, as well as myself,

00:07:48.711 --> 00:07:50.398
we run membership masterminds.

00:07:50.398 --> 00:07:52.126
He runs the School of Greatness.

00:07:52.126 --> 00:07:53.719
I run Fire Nation Elite.

00:07:53.719 --> 00:07:55.719
These are membership masterminds that are

00:07:55.719 --> 00:07:57.278
around 100 people each.

00:07:57.278 --> 00:08:00.049
And so it's a lot less expensive.

00:08:00.049 --> 00:08:02.077
Whenever people reach out to me and say

00:08:02.077 --> 00:08:04.406
"I'm doubting what I'm doing,"

00:08:04.406 --> 00:08:07.509
I say "Well, congratulations,
you're a human being."

00:08:07.509 --> 00:08:08.668
That's what it means to be a human.

00:08:08.668 --> 00:08:09.501
- High five.

00:08:09.501 --> 00:08:12.377
- You doubt what you do, it's
that voice inside our head.

00:08:12.377 --> 00:08:14.260
It's innate in all of us.

00:08:14.260 --> 00:08:16.261
We're born with the Imposter Syndrome,

00:08:16.261 --> 00:08:18.926
so we don't walk outside
of the cave at dark

00:08:18.926 --> 00:08:19.807
with a saber-toothed tiger.

00:08:19.807 --> 00:08:20.823
It's like there's a voice saying

00:08:20.823 --> 00:08:22.389
"Don't walk outside, it's dangerous."

00:08:22.389 --> 00:08:24.607
But we don't live in
that same world anymore.

00:08:24.607 --> 00:08:27.648
But we still have that inside
our head, and that's fine,

00:08:27.648 --> 00:08:30.037
but we need to learn not
to let it control our lives

00:08:30.037 --> 00:08:31.619
but to embrace that fear.

00:08:31.619 --> 00:08:33.842
And so I still have it, every single day.

00:08:33.842 --> 00:08:36.211
We talked about my revenue last month.

00:08:36.211 --> 00:08:40.221
I still wake up and say
"Man, should I be doing,

00:08:40.221 --> 00:08:42.636
should I do this webinar?"

00:08:42.636 --> 00:08:44.597
I have these doubts and these fears.

00:08:44.597 --> 00:08:47.268
That's just a reality and I've
just learned to embrace that.

00:08:47.268 --> 00:08:52.139
This quote is by the
ex-President Theodore Roosevelt.

00:08:52.139 --> 00:08:55.830
He delivered this in Paris back in 1910.

00:08:55.830 --> 00:08:59.554
"It is not the critic who counts,
not the man who points out

00:08:59.554 --> 00:09:02.462
how the strong man stumbles,

00:09:02.462 --> 00:09:06.168
or where the doer of deeds
could have done better.

00:09:06.168 --> 00:09:08.798
Credit belongs to the man
who is actually in the arena,

00:09:08.798 --> 00:09:12.766
whose face is marred by
blood, by dirt, by sweat,

00:09:12.766 --> 00:09:16.260
who strives valiantly, who
errs, who comes up short

00:09:16.260 --> 00:09:18.141
again and again,

00:09:18.141 --> 00:09:22.503
because there is no effort
without error and shortcoming,

00:09:22.503 --> 00:09:26.300
but who does actually
strive to do the deeds,

00:09:26.300 --> 00:09:29.690
who has great enthusiasms
and great devotions

00:09:29.690 --> 00:09:32.799
and spends himself on a worthy cause.

00:09:32.799 --> 00:09:36.677
Who, at the best in the
end, knows high achievement,

00:09:36.677 --> 00:09:39.942
and at the worst, if he fails,

00:09:39.942 --> 00:09:41.942
he fails daring greatly,

00:09:42.877 --> 00:09:44.890
so that his place will never be with

00:09:44.890 --> 00:09:47.201
those cold and timid souls

00:09:47.201 --> 00:09:50.751
who know neither victory nor defeat."

00:09:50.751 --> 00:09:53.302
I had this mentor, Jamie, and I was in

00:09:53.302 --> 00:09:56.163
Cliff Ravenscraft's broadcast mastermind,

00:09:56.163 --> 00:09:58.008
and they said "John, you're insane,

00:09:58.008 --> 00:09:59.429
it's going to be so much work.

00:09:59.429 --> 00:10:00.982
Don't do a seven-day-a-week podcast,

00:10:00.982 --> 00:10:02.740
do a one-day-a-week podcast."

00:10:02.740 --> 00:10:04.649
And I think this is really important too,

00:10:04.649 --> 00:10:06.990
is that you need, as an entrepreneur,

00:10:06.990 --> 00:10:11.449
to really take the great
feedback and the great advice

00:10:11.449 --> 00:10:13.181
from those who have come before you,

00:10:13.181 --> 00:10:14.770
from those who are mentoring you,

00:10:14.770 --> 00:10:15.603
but at the same time you
need to listen to your gut,

00:10:15.603 --> 00:10:17.103
to your intuition.

00:10:18.211 --> 00:10:19.970
I took so many great pieces of advice

00:10:19.970 --> 00:10:22.751
from both Cliff and Jamie and
applied it to my business,

00:10:22.751 --> 00:10:25.990
but when it came to a
seven-day-a-week podcast,

00:10:25.990 --> 00:10:28.503
my intuition, my gut was telling me

00:10:28.503 --> 00:10:30.946
it had to be that way.

00:10:30.946 --> 00:10:33.589
That's what I was meant to create.

00:10:33.589 --> 00:10:35.959
I had the military discipline,

00:10:35.959 --> 00:10:38.372
I knew how to create systems,

00:10:38.372 --> 00:10:39.460
I knew how to work hard.

00:10:39.460 --> 00:10:41.869
I had the finances to allow me to do so

00:10:41.869 --> 00:10:43.570
for a significant amount of time

00:10:43.570 --> 00:10:45.760
without being distracted by other things.

00:10:45.760 --> 00:10:47.699
I knew that I could make it happen,

00:10:47.699 --> 00:10:50.612
I knew that was going to be my USP.

00:10:50.612 --> 00:10:53.825
that unique selling
proposition, my differentiator.

00:10:53.825 --> 00:10:56.038
So I listened to my heart,
I listened to my gut,

00:10:56.038 --> 00:10:59.103
and I think that's so
important for people to realize

00:10:59.103 --> 00:11:01.110
that you're going to have
something in your gut

00:11:01.110 --> 00:11:02.851
that's telling you to do something

00:11:02.851 --> 00:11:04.858
and hopefully it's
something that is unique

00:11:04.858 --> 00:11:05.792
and it is different.

00:11:05.792 --> 00:11:07.284
And make sure you follow that.

00:11:07.284 --> 00:11:08.909
- I think a lot of people
that are going to watch this,

00:11:08.909 --> 00:11:12.313
that are watching, that start
podcasts or have podcasts,

00:11:12.313 --> 00:11:13.942
or want to start them.

00:11:13.942 --> 00:11:17.559
So what's kind of the
right way to do it for you?

00:11:17.559 --> 00:11:21.339
- If you are willing to
step back and say "Hey,

00:11:21.339 --> 00:11:25.170
I am going to commit to
delivering free, valuable

00:11:25.170 --> 00:11:27.370
and consistent content,"
those three things.

00:11:27.370 --> 00:11:30.817
"I'm going to commit to
delivering free, valuable,

00:11:30.817 --> 00:11:33.279
and consistent content," you are going

00:11:33.279 --> 00:11:35.398
to grow an audience in your niche.

00:11:35.398 --> 00:11:38.289
If you can commit to getting
to that tipping point,

00:11:38.289 --> 00:11:39.612
then that tipping point's going to happen

00:11:39.612 --> 00:11:42.036
when you're doing the free,
valuable and consistent content.

00:11:42.036 --> 00:11:43.602
You will get to revenue,

00:11:43.602 --> 00:11:46.378
but it's not going to come
overnight, over one month.

00:11:46.378 --> 00:11:47.975
It's going to take time, and that's why

00:11:47.975 --> 00:11:49.488
I'm not against a side hustle.

00:11:49.488 --> 00:11:51.170
I want to talk for just a minute about

00:11:51.170 --> 00:11:53.539
action versus reaction.

00:11:53.539 --> 00:11:55.498
Such a critical concept.

00:11:55.498 --> 00:11:58.160
So many people start their
day in reaction mode.

00:11:58.160 --> 00:12:00.468
"Let me grab my phone,
let me see what's on X,

00:12:00.468 --> 00:12:02.357
let's see what's on Y,
let's see what's on Z.

00:12:02.357 --> 00:12:05.369
Facebook, what's in my email,
what do people want from me?"

00:12:05.369 --> 00:12:06.908
If you start in reaction
mode, you're likely

00:12:06.908 --> 00:12:08.849
going to end in reaction mode.

00:12:08.849 --> 00:12:09.769
No bueno.

00:12:09.769 --> 00:12:12.421
We don't want to start
or end in reaction mode.

00:12:12.421 --> 00:12:14.086
How about this, we're going
to start in action mode.

00:12:14.086 --> 00:12:15.119
We're going to take actions

00:12:15.119 --> 00:12:15.952
that are going to improve our business,

00:12:15.952 --> 00:12:20.744
add value to what we're bringing
in value to other's lives.

00:12:20.744 --> 00:12:23.019
When my mental bandwidth is the highest,

00:12:23.019 --> 00:12:24.349
that's when I go into action mode,

00:12:24.349 --> 00:12:25.182
first thing in the morning.

00:12:25.182 --> 00:12:29.509
The first hour is me producing
my best content, period.

00:12:29.509 --> 00:12:32.130
I'm not going to spend
my best mental bandwidth

00:12:32.130 --> 00:12:34.145
answering somebody's
emails, someone's questions,

00:12:34.145 --> 00:12:36.570
someone's ask, want, need.

00:12:36.570 --> 00:12:39.500
No, it's your best mental bandwidth.

00:12:39.500 --> 00:12:40.736
Reaction's part of the game though,

00:12:40.736 --> 00:12:42.558
realize you do need to engage,

00:12:42.558 --> 00:12:44.877
you do need to answer
those emails, those tweets,

00:12:44.877 --> 00:12:45.821
those Facebook messages.

00:12:45.821 --> 00:12:46.757
Did you Snapchat me?

00:12:46.757 --> 00:12:49.540
I'm answering back, that
is part of the game.

00:12:49.540 --> 00:12:52.057
Start to be intentional
about when you act,

00:12:52.057 --> 00:12:53.297
and when you react.

00:12:53.297 --> 00:12:55.489
And remember, use your
best mental bandwidth,

00:12:55.489 --> 00:12:57.723
your energy, for action.

00:12:57.723 --> 00:13:00.161
Fire Nation, as entrepreneurs, we love

00:13:00.161 --> 00:13:02.477
getting right to the finish line

00:13:02.477 --> 00:13:04.728
and then never crossing it.

00:13:04.728 --> 00:13:06.548
You want to know why?

00:13:06.548 --> 00:13:09.938
Because as long as we're
living in the pre-launch mode,

00:13:09.938 --> 00:13:13.686
we're living in the fairy
world of "this might work,

00:13:13.686 --> 00:13:16.336
it might work," but as
soon as you press Publish

00:13:16.336 --> 00:13:19.096
thn it gets real, then
the market tells you

00:13:19.096 --> 00:13:20.828
if this is going to work or not.

00:13:20.828 --> 00:13:23.699
You either win or you lose.

00:13:23.699 --> 00:13:25.847
And we are terrified of losing.

00:13:25.847 --> 00:13:28.009
We want to live in that
comfortable pre-launch stage

00:13:28.009 --> 00:13:31.759
of where everything and
anything is possible.

00:13:33.695 --> 00:13:37.169
Fire Nation, everybody told
me EOFire would not work,

00:13:37.169 --> 00:13:39.426
that a daily podcast was doomed to fail,

00:13:39.426 --> 00:13:41.088
so I kept pushing back my
launch so I could live in

00:13:41.088 --> 00:13:43.298
my fairy world of this might.

00:13:43.298 --> 00:13:45.508
I wanted my dream to stay
alive a little longer,

00:13:45.508 --> 00:13:48.068
until I pushed that go
button, that publish button,

00:13:48.068 --> 00:13:50.868
EOFire was still possibly a success.

00:13:50.868 --> 00:13:52.718
Reality hadn't set in.

00:13:52.718 --> 00:13:56.729
So yes, your chances of losing
are a lot less pre-launch,

00:13:56.729 --> 00:14:00.037
but your chances of winning are zero.

00:14:00.037 --> 00:14:02.677
So take this away, Fire Nation.

00:14:02.677 --> 00:14:05.708
You will lose if you remain
in your fairy-tele land

00:14:05.708 --> 00:14:08.468
of pre-launch, and you will never win.

00:14:08.468 --> 00:14:10.461
There's one way to win: press Launch.

00:14:10.461 --> 00:14:12.240
Let the market decide.

00:14:12.240 --> 00:14:15.088
The alarm clock would go off very early,

00:14:15.088 --> 00:14:16.118
every single morning.

00:14:16.118 --> 00:14:18.834
I'd wake up, I'd check
my phone immediately

00:14:18.834 --> 00:14:21.114
for any emails that had come in,

00:14:21.114 --> 00:14:25.812
and then it'd be shower,
shave, dress in my full suit,

00:14:25.812 --> 00:14:27.220
and jump in a car.

00:14:27.220 --> 00:14:29.368
And that car, to me, represented

00:14:29.368 --> 00:14:33.518
my first kind of drudgery
task for the day.

00:14:33.518 --> 00:14:35.408
It made me feel trapped because

00:14:35.408 --> 00:14:36.888
I had done all the right things.

00:14:36.888 --> 00:14:38.969
I had gone to college, I had been

00:14:38.969 --> 00:14:41.506
an officer in the Army for eight years,

00:14:41.506 --> 00:14:43.570
I had gone to corporate finance.

00:14:43.570 --> 00:14:44.868
I had worked with John Hancock,

00:14:44.868 --> 00:14:46.350
I'd done commercial real estate,

00:14:46.350 --> 00:14:50.379
I had done all the right
things, and now here I was,

00:14:50.379 --> 00:14:52.800
stuck on this hamster wheel going nowhere,

00:14:52.800 --> 00:14:56.219
inspiring nobody, especially not myself,

00:14:56.219 --> 00:14:58.198
and just feeling like I was trapped

00:14:58.198 --> 00:15:00.355
in this world that I had created.

00:15:00.355 --> 00:15:01.966
And it was a sinking pit in my stomach,

00:15:01.966 --> 00:15:04.108
and I just was wondering "Why?

00:15:04.108 --> 00:15:05.858
Why have I not found what I'm looking for

00:15:05.858 --> 00:15:07.469
when I've done everything that I was told

00:15:07.469 --> 00:15:09.048
I was supposed to do?

00:15:09.048 --> 00:15:12.680
Here I am, years later,
on the same hamster wheel,

00:15:12.680 --> 00:15:15.115
feeling like I'm even further behind

00:15:15.115 --> 00:15:17.468
where I thought I was five years ago."

00:15:17.468 --> 00:15:20.429
And this sinking mundane
feeling would just not go away.

00:15:20.429 --> 00:15:23.004
It was ever-present with me,

00:15:23.004 --> 00:15:25.171
and I just wanted freedom.

00:15:27.829 --> 00:15:29.203
And I just wanted freedom.

00:15:29.203 --> 00:15:31.848
I just wanted to do work
that I was inspired by,

00:15:31.848 --> 00:15:34.279
and I just wanted to do
work that inspired others.

00:15:34.279 --> 00:15:37.446
(inspirational music)

00:15:44.148 --> 00:15:47.198
So there I was, stuck in traffic again.

00:15:47.198 --> 00:15:50.622
But at least I had my
podcasts, or so I thought.

00:15:50.622 --> 00:15:52.960
Then, before I knew it, poof.

00:15:52.960 --> 00:15:56.708
My last podcast had ended,
and I scrolled through my iPod

00:15:56.708 --> 00:15:58.463
and there was nothing left.

00:15:58.463 --> 00:16:01.200
And I thought to myself,
"Man, I have to sit here

00:16:01.200 --> 00:16:03.300
for another 45 minutes in traffic,

00:16:03.300 --> 00:16:04.918
with nothing but the radio?"

00:16:04.918 --> 00:16:08.007
I was devastated, but at that moment,

00:16:08.007 --> 00:16:11.170
at that devastation moment, that is truly

00:16:11.170 --> 00:16:12.737
when my "ah-ha" moment happened.

00:16:12.737 --> 00:16:14.168
That's when the light bulb went off.

00:16:14.168 --> 00:16:15.350
I said "What?

00:16:15.350 --> 00:16:17.158
There's not a seven-day-a-week podcast?

00:16:17.158 --> 00:16:19.168
There should be a podcast that comes out

00:16:19.168 --> 00:16:21.099
every single day of the week,

00:16:21.099 --> 00:16:24.170
that interviews inspiring and
successful entrepreneurs."

00:16:24.170 --> 00:16:26.579
And that's when I had
my light bulb moment,

00:16:26.579 --> 00:16:28.799
I said "There should be
someone that creates it,

00:16:28.799 --> 00:16:30.145
why not me?

00:16:30.145 --> 00:16:31.630
I know all these great entrepreneurs

00:16:31.630 --> 00:16:33.006
who are hosts of their own shows,

00:16:33.006 --> 00:16:36.049
I could be just like them, I can do this."

00:16:36.049 --> 00:16:39.330
So I had all of these
thoughts swirling in my head.

00:16:39.330 --> 00:16:41.922
"Am I going to fail, am I
going to look like a fool

00:16:41.922 --> 00:16:44.256
in front of my friends and
family, who up to this point

00:16:44.256 --> 00:16:46.645
had only seen me as a success."

00:16:46.645 --> 00:16:47.988
And these were real fears.

00:16:47.988 --> 00:16:50.618
I used to wake up sweating
in the middle of the night,

00:16:50.618 --> 00:16:54.110
saying "What am I doing, this is insane."

00:16:54.110 --> 00:16:56.914
And the only way that I would
be able to go back to sleep

00:16:56.914 --> 00:16:59.679
was by convincing myself that
when I woke up in the morning,

00:16:59.679 --> 00:17:02.768
I was going to give it all up
and go crawling back to my job

00:17:02.768 --> 00:17:04.067
in commercial real estate.

00:17:04.067 --> 00:17:06.480
But luckily for me, I would
wake up in the morning,

00:17:06.480 --> 00:17:08.948
the sun would be shining,
and I'd say "You know what,

00:17:08.948 --> 00:17:11.447
I'm going to give it one more day.

00:17:11.447 --> 00:17:13.890
One more day, I'm going to sit down.

00:17:13.890 --> 00:17:16.726
I'm going to learn what it
takes to upload a media file.

00:17:16.726 --> 00:17:19.540
I'm going to learn how
to create great audio.

00:17:19.540 --> 00:17:21.259
I'm going to learn what it
takes to be an interviewer,

00:17:21.259 --> 00:17:23.349
a host of a show."

00:17:23.349 --> 00:17:26.328
And every single day
I would get shot down,

00:17:26.328 --> 00:17:29.576
I would struggle, I would
come up against another wall

00:17:29.576 --> 00:17:31.180
that would knock me down,

00:17:31.180 --> 00:17:34.363
but I kept getting up, I
kept taking one step forward

00:17:34.363 --> 00:17:36.828
for every half-step
that I was taking back.

00:17:36.828 --> 00:17:39.561
And slowly but surely,
I saw myself turning in

00:17:39.561 --> 00:17:42.099
to a podcaster, right
before my very own eyes.

00:17:42.099 --> 00:17:45.258
And it was a slow process, but it did come

00:17:45.258 --> 00:17:48.936
and I was able to
continue to move forward,

00:17:48.936 --> 00:17:52.537
until the launch of Entrepreneur
On Fire on September 22nd,

00:17:52.537 --> 00:17:56.537
cause at that point,
there was no turning back.

00:17:57.930 --> 00:18:00.025
Fire Nation, look what we've created.

00:18:00.025 --> 00:18:02.320
Look at the inspiration we've spread

00:18:02.320 --> 00:18:03.761
throughout this world.

00:18:03.761 --> 00:18:07.429
A podcast, seven days a
week, interviewing the most

00:18:07.429 --> 00:18:10.884
inspiring and successful
entrepreneurs in the world.

00:18:10.884 --> 00:18:15.669
Seth Godin, Guy Kawasaki,
Tim Ferriss, Gary Vaynerchuk.

00:18:15.669 --> 00:18:18.920
We are listened to in over 145 countries,

00:18:18.920 --> 00:18:21.842
millions and millions of unique downloads.

00:18:21.842 --> 00:18:24.090
Fire Nation, look at what we've created.

00:18:24.090 --> 00:18:25.820
Look at what we've done.

00:18:25.820 --> 00:18:27.329
And we've just started.

00:18:27.329 --> 00:18:30.496
(inspirational music)

00:18:39.876 --> 00:18:41.580
- Thank you guys so much for watching.

00:18:41.580 --> 00:18:44.649
I made this video because
Ethan Pariseau asked me to,

00:18:44.649 --> 00:18:46.262
so if there's a famous entrepreneur that

00:18:46.262 --> 00:18:47.762
you want me to profile next,

00:18:47.762 --> 00:18:49.899
leave it in the comments below
and I'll see what I can do.

00:18:49.899 --> 00:18:51.690
I'd also love to know, what did John say

00:18:51.690 --> 00:18:53.570
that really resonated with you,

00:18:53.570 --> 00:18:55.509
what lessons are you
going to take and apply

00:18:55.509 --> 00:18:57.099
to your life or to your business?

00:18:57.099 --> 00:18:58.080
Leave it in the comments

00:18:58.080 --> 00:18:59.645
and I'm going to join in the discussion.

00:18:59.645 --> 00:19:01.038
Thank you guys again for watching.

00:19:01.038 --> 00:19:04.407
I believe in you, I hope you
continue to believe in yourself

00:19:04.407 --> 00:19:06.469
and whatever your one word is.

00:19:06.469 --> 00:19:08.886
Much love, I'll see you soon.

